1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,130

2
00:00:06,130 --> 00:00:09,640
We're glad to have Angela
Fan today with us here.

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:14,890
And she's a research scientist
at Meta AI Research in New York,

4
00:00:14,890 --> 00:00:17,770
focusing on research in
text generation mainly.

5
00:00:17,770 --> 00:00:20,500
And currently, she's
working on language modeling

6
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,400
and developing the line
AI agents meta products.

7
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:27,520
And recent research products
include No Language Left

8
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:30,580
Behind, which we'll be
talking briefly about today,

9
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:33,340
universal speech translation
for unwritten languages

10
00:00:33,340 --> 00:00:35,440
as well as Llama2.

11
00:00:35,440 --> 00:00:39,520
So give it up for
Angela, I guess.

12
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:41,260
All right, thank
you all so much.

13
00:00:41,260 --> 00:00:43,282
So yeah, when I got this
email, I was like, oh,

14
00:00:43,282 --> 00:00:44,740
I should probably
talk about lama2,

15
00:00:44,740 --> 00:00:46,330
but then I noticed
you have Sharon,

16
00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:49,210
who is like a 10x
better speaker than me.

17
00:00:49,210 --> 00:00:51,670
So I was like, OK,
maybe not Llama2.

18
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:54,490
But then I thought I maybe would
cover this project that we did

19
00:00:54,490 --> 00:00:57,820
called No Language Left
Behind, which could be also

20
00:00:57,820 --> 00:00:59,470
very relevant to this class.

21
00:00:59,470 --> 00:01:03,100
And so when you think about
a lot of text generation

22
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:07,700
technology, most of it,
until fairly recently,

23
00:01:07,700 --> 00:01:09,950
has been really
focused on English.

24
00:01:09,950 --> 00:01:13,400
But there are actually more
than 3,000 written languages

25
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:14,240
worldwide.

26
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,730
And for me, this is extremely
personally meaningful

27
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:19,790
because, actually, English
is my third language.

28
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:22,770
So it's really important--

29
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:23,270
yeah.

30
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:26,540
So it's really also very
personally meaningful.

31
00:01:26,540 --> 00:01:30,110
And when you think about some of
the multilingual technology that

32
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:33,290
permeates, it's not like we've
never worked on multilingual.

33
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:35,900
Actually, when speaking
about generative AI,

34
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:37,670
I actually think
translation is one

35
00:01:37,670 --> 00:01:40,310
of the most commercially
successful and widespread

36
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:42,020
applications of generative AI.

37
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:43,970
I mean, ultimately
translation models.

38
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:46,880
They are like conditional
language models.

39
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,060
And so when you think about
traveling or something

40
00:01:50,060 --> 00:01:53,180
like that, or my sister
is taking Spanish, so just

41
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:54,740
like doing her
Spanish homework, we

42
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:56,480
have a lot of tools
that exist today.

43
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,140
So things like Google Translate
cover around 130 languages,

44
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:01,970
Microsoft Translate about 110.

45
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:03,470
This might be a
little bit outdated,

46
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:07,160
since I pulled the
statistics a little bit ago.

47
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:09,020
But the project for
No Language Left

48
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,270
Behind, it started
from a very simple ask.

49
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:13,790
OK, there's 3,000
languages worldwide.

50
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:17,120
Maybe it'll be like pretty
hard to get to all 3,000,

51
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:21,530
since some of them are pretty
rare, and not spoken by many.

52
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:25,320
But there are still hundreds
of languages spoken by millions

53
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:26,730
and millions of people.

54
00:02:26,730 --> 00:02:29,310
And so we were like,
OK, no big deal.

55
00:02:29,310 --> 00:02:32,280
Let's just start from the
100-ish that we have today

56
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,355
and just go for like a doubling.

57
00:02:34,355 --> 00:02:35,730
What would it take
to actually be

58
00:02:35,730 --> 00:02:38,100
able to double this
kind of coverage?

59
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:41,310
And, of course, just saying that
you support a bunch of languages

60
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:42,570
is not the goal.

61
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:45,600
We actually want to create
high-quality safe translations

62
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:47,310
that would be usable by people.

63
00:02:47,310 --> 00:02:49,560
Just like if you're
going on vacation today,

64
00:02:49,560 --> 00:02:51,420
you're instinct is to
whip out your phone

65
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:54,290
and get on the
Google Translate app.

66
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:57,110
And so the backdrop
to this project

67
00:02:57,110 --> 00:02:59,960
was that there was actually a
lot of progress in translation.

68
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,180
So historically, there's
been a lot of focus

69
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:04,310
on what we call higher
resource languages,

70
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:06,080
and these are not
necessarily languages

71
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:08,300
that are spoken by the
most people in the world.

72
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:09,980
But when we say
higher resource, it

73
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:11,810
means the most amount of data.

74
00:03:11,810 --> 00:03:13,280
And so you can
think about things

75
00:03:13,280 --> 00:03:17,450
like Euro Parl or translations
from the European parliament.

76
00:03:17,450 --> 00:03:19,220
And those served
as the foundation

77
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:21,890
for a lot of
translation development.

78
00:03:21,890 --> 00:03:23,990
And more recently,
there's been a great focus

79
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:25,490
on low-resource languages.

80
00:03:25,490 --> 00:03:28,100
And it's been driven across
the research community

81
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:31,970
with groups like Ghana NLP,
Masakhane, America's NLP.

82
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:34,430
And these are all really
exciting developments.

83
00:03:34,430 --> 00:03:37,490
And so these have led to a
lot of development of new data

84
00:03:37,490 --> 00:03:40,910
sets as well as criticisms
of existing data

85
00:03:40,910 --> 00:03:43,430
sets, and also work
on new languages,

86
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:45,560
and usually, languages
that people speak

87
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:46,790
and they care a lot about.

88
00:03:46,790 --> 00:03:49,460
And we found this
really, really exciting.

89
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:51,650
And so looking at a lot
of this, a bunch of us

90
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:54,630
got together at fair and
started thinking like,

91
00:03:54,630 --> 00:03:57,840
OK, we actually speak some
pretty low-resource languages

92
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,930
from Catalan to
Assamese and so on.

93
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:03,360
And so we started this as
kind of like a big passionate

94
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:04,410
research project.

95
00:04:04,410 --> 00:04:06,630
And so today, I want
to cover a little bit

96
00:04:06,630 --> 00:04:09,840
about our high-level approach
to this problem, which is

97
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,490
a little bit interdisciplinary.

98
00:04:11,490 --> 00:04:13,200
I want to talk about
how we actually

99
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:17,279
created the data sets to be able
to support this kind of work.

100
00:04:17,279 --> 00:04:19,380
Of course, I want to
talk about the models,

101
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:21,600
since this is a class
about transformers.

102
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:23,203
One note here, I
think that's actually

103
00:04:23,203 --> 00:04:24,870
very interesting in
terms of translation

104
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:28,410
as a research direction is that
actually, a lot of innovations

105
00:04:28,410 --> 00:04:31,237
have been done in translation.

106
00:04:31,237 --> 00:04:33,570
The original transformer
paper, I think, is one of them,

107
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:36,480
and which makes always
translation a quite interesting

108
00:04:36,480 --> 00:04:39,840
area to work on because I feel
like it's a very mature research

109
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:40,840
area as well.

110
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:42,990
So it is like, OK,
if your architecture

111
00:04:42,990 --> 00:04:45,310
works in translation, it
probably works very generally.

112
00:04:45,310 --> 00:04:47,310
So that's also one of the
things that excites me

113
00:04:47,310 --> 00:04:49,668
about translation research.

114
00:04:49,668 --> 00:04:51,210
Then I want to talk
about evaluation,

115
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:53,250
like how are we actually
measuring and ensuring

116
00:04:53,250 --> 00:04:54,780
the quality of
these translations

117
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,880
are good and safe for people.

118
00:04:56,880 --> 00:04:58,920
And then I want to
end with a little bit

119
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:02,550
of high-level thoughts about
future directions and things

120
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:05,340
that I hope that we can
work on in the future.

121
00:05:05,340 --> 00:05:07,980
So I want to start
with our approach.

122
00:05:07,980 --> 00:05:09,930
I think the most important
thing in research

123
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:12,330
is to know that we're
working on a real problem,

124
00:05:12,330 --> 00:05:15,240
especially when it's
really close to people,

125
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:16,410
like translation.

126
00:05:16,410 --> 00:05:18,540
And I think in many
areas, like when

127
00:05:18,540 --> 00:05:20,460
I was working on
On-Device AI, for example,

128
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:22,890
I feel like I had like a
research problem in mind,

129
00:05:22,890 --> 00:05:25,050
but it was very,
very disconnected

130
00:05:25,050 --> 00:05:27,390
from the practical problem
of actually putting models

131
00:05:27,390 --> 00:05:28,110
on phones.

132
00:05:28,110 --> 00:05:30,600
And so this was something that
was really important to us.

133
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,730
And so we actually
started the project

134
00:05:32,730 --> 00:05:36,450
by focusing on a
social-sciences-type approach

135
00:05:36,450 --> 00:05:39,000
or sociology-type approach.

136
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,980
And we actually did
a lot of interviews

137
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:42,660
with low-resource speakers.

138
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:46,320
And so we met with about 44
different native speakers

139
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:50,370
that spoke 36 different
languages across North America.

140
00:05:50,370 --> 00:05:53,250
I will say that a lot of them
are immigrants to the US,

141
00:05:53,250 --> 00:05:57,020
since that was the easiest
kind of cohort to recruit.

142
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:59,090
And we learned a lot
of different things

143
00:05:59,090 --> 00:06:01,700
about how they approach
low-resource languages,

144
00:06:01,700 --> 00:06:04,300
but also the technological
need that they have.

145
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:06,050
Because I think it's
easy to be like, hey,

146
00:06:06,050 --> 00:06:07,968
I have this cool background.

147
00:06:07,968 --> 00:06:10,010
I have this cool problem,
and I want to solve it.

148
00:06:10,010 --> 00:06:12,635
But I think it's very important
to actually talk to the people.

149
00:06:12,635 --> 00:06:14,870
This is a problem that
needs to be solved.

150
00:06:14,870 --> 00:06:17,960
And so we learned that there's
great fear, in general,

151
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:20,240
that low-resource languages
might be undergoing

152
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:23,570
a state of decline, partially
because a lot of education

153
00:06:23,570 --> 00:06:28,290
is shifting to languages
like Hindi or English

154
00:06:28,290 --> 00:06:30,410
or Mandarin Chinese,
for example.

155
00:06:30,410 --> 00:06:33,410
And there's a lot of excitement
to be included in existing

156
00:06:33,410 --> 00:06:34,460
translation systems.

157
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:35,840
And people said,
they have always

158
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:39,410
tried to use Google Translate
or Microsoft Translate

159
00:06:39,410 --> 00:06:40,880
in their existing languages.

160
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:42,740
But ultimately, they
found that the quality

161
00:06:42,740 --> 00:06:45,140
is really insufficient
for reliable usage.

162
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:46,555
So if you think about--

163
00:06:46,555 --> 00:06:48,680
well, I was going to say
when I was in high school,

164
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:51,180
but you all are probably like
substantially younger than me.

165
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:53,600
So maybe 10 or so
years ago and you

166
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:56,070
try to use Google Translate
for your Spanish homework,

167
00:06:56,070 --> 00:06:57,445
your Spanish
teacher could always

168
00:06:57,445 --> 00:07:00,522
identify that it was not a
human-written translation.

169
00:07:00,522 --> 00:07:02,730
And so you would get marks
off, but that's not really

170
00:07:02,730 --> 00:07:05,790
the case for some of the
high-resource languages today.

171
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:08,580
And so I think, as with all
things in machine learning,

172
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:10,680
it really starts from
a data perspective.

173
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,600
Why can't we just train
models in hundreds

174
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,270
of languages or large language
models in hundreds of languages?

175
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:17,650
It's because we don't have
the data to support it.

176
00:07:17,650 --> 00:07:20,640
And so I want to talk
first about evaluation data

177
00:07:20,640 --> 00:07:22,560
sets because I think
it's extremely important

178
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:24,570
to nail evaluation.

179
00:07:24,570 --> 00:07:26,850
And then I'll talk
about training.

180
00:07:26,850 --> 00:07:31,260
So for an evaluation data
set for this work we started,

181
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:34,030
this FLORES effort, it stands
for Facebook Low Resource.

182
00:07:34,030 --> 00:07:35,530
I guess we're called
Meta now, but I

183
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:39,210
didn't think FLORES was
a very good renaming.

184
00:07:39,210 --> 00:07:40,830
So we'll still
calling it FLORES.

185
00:07:40,830 --> 00:07:42,390
So this was something
we originally

186
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:47,340
started for just two languages
in this first paper, EMNLP,

187
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:48,300
many years ago.

188
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:50,340
So it was just for
Nepali and Sinhala,

189
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:52,710
and we later extended it
to incorporate two more

190
00:07:52,710 --> 00:07:54,160
languages in a release.

191
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:57,160
Afterwards, we thought
a lot about OK,

192
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:00,130
FLORES was really useful
for the community.

193
00:08:00,130 --> 00:08:02,200
How can we extend
it to 100 languages?

194
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:04,390
And so that was his
follow-up work that we did.

195
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:07,720
I think we had an ACL or WMT.

196
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:10,030
And then in this project,
we were like, OK,

197
00:08:10,030 --> 00:08:14,710
how can we go from FLORES-101
to FLORES-200 to really go

198
00:08:14,710 --> 00:08:16,330
for the doubling effect?

199
00:08:16,330 --> 00:08:17,680
And so what is FLORES?

200
00:08:17,680 --> 00:08:18,880
Well, it's in the name.

201
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:21,220
It's a focus on
low-resource languages.

202
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:24,040
So we do include some
higher resource languages

203
00:08:24,040 --> 00:08:28,030
like German or Hindi or so on,
almost for calibration effect

204
00:08:28,030 --> 00:08:28,750
as well.

205
00:08:28,750 --> 00:08:31,450
But the majority of the
focus is on these lower

206
00:08:31,450 --> 00:08:33,010
and mid-resource languages.

207
00:08:33,010 --> 00:08:37,450
It's the first large-scale,
many-to-many machine translation

208
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:40,122
evaluation data set, which
means that we take all

209
00:08:40,122 --> 00:08:41,289
of the sentences in English.

210
00:08:41,289 --> 00:08:43,929
And then we translate them to
all of the languages, which

211
00:08:43,929 --> 00:08:45,850
means that you would
be able to evaluate

212
00:08:45,850 --> 00:08:48,190
any cross pair of languages.

213
00:08:48,190 --> 00:08:50,290
So for example, like
Chinese to French.

214
00:08:50,290 --> 00:08:52,060
I lived in France
for many years,

215
00:08:52,060 --> 00:08:54,020
so it's very personally
relevant to me.

216
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:56,480
Of course, 200 languages,
also in the name.

217
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:59,690
There's a broad diversity of
different domains and topics.

218
00:08:59,690 --> 00:09:02,830
I think this is important when
designing an evaluation data

219
00:09:02,830 --> 00:09:05,810
set, which is very top-of-mind
for anybody interested

220
00:09:05,810 --> 00:09:10,580
in language modeling research,
because the way people train

221
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:12,890
machine translation
models and the way people

222
00:09:12,890 --> 00:09:15,110
use them are often
very different.

223
00:09:15,110 --> 00:09:17,990
And so if you only benchmark
your data set, for example,

224
00:09:17,990 --> 00:09:20,457
on news, which is very common
in translation research,

225
00:09:20,457 --> 00:09:22,040
then you don't really
pick up the fact

226
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:25,380
that people talk about such
a wide variety of things

227
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:27,260
and have different
casual conversations

228
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:30,710
that they need translated,
official documents, and so on.

229
00:09:30,710 --> 00:09:33,170
It's also
document-level data set.

230
00:09:33,170 --> 00:09:35,690
This is not something that I
think the community is broadly

231
00:09:35,690 --> 00:09:38,090
leveraging right now, but
the way it's translated

232
00:09:38,090 --> 00:09:40,610
is that you can have
document-level context.

233
00:09:40,610 --> 00:09:43,340
And so translators are
provided the entire document

234
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:44,300
to translate from.

235
00:09:44,300 --> 00:09:47,330
And we also provide the entire
document for evaluation.

236
00:09:47,330 --> 00:09:49,400
And we translate
multiple sentences

237
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:50,540
from the same paragraph.

238
00:09:50,540 --> 00:09:53,265
And so this was like a
potential research direction

239
00:09:53,265 --> 00:09:54,890
that we wanted to
make sure we covered,

240
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:57,290
models that needed
potentially more context,

241
00:09:57,290 --> 00:09:58,700
because a lot of
translation work

242
00:09:58,700 --> 00:10:00,710
is done at the sentence level.

243
00:10:00,710 --> 00:10:02,720
So how do we actually
ensure that this data

244
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:03,860
set was high-quality?

245
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:07,370
So the first step is
that we take a document.

246
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:10,130
Well, actually, first step is
alignment on language standards.

247
00:10:10,130 --> 00:10:12,320
So this is very
important because when

248
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:14,690
you're translating
French or Chinese,

249
00:10:14,690 --> 00:10:17,870
I think most people have a
strong understanding of what

250
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:20,540
it means to produce a good
French or good Chinese.

251
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:22,670
And there are a lot of
professional translators

252
00:10:22,670 --> 00:10:23,990
hired in these languages.

253
00:10:23,990 --> 00:10:26,420
But when you go to lower
resource languages,

254
00:10:26,420 --> 00:10:28,430
it's not necessarily
the case that there's

255
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:31,190
like a glowing
translation industry

256
00:10:31,190 --> 00:10:35,240
around translating a
lower resource language.

257
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:37,130
And so one of the first
things is actually

258
00:10:37,130 --> 00:10:39,890
to align on like, what is
a high-quality translation?

259
00:10:39,890 --> 00:10:42,407
And so there's actually
a lot of challenges here.

260
00:10:42,407 --> 00:10:44,240
So there are certain
low-resource languages,

261
00:10:44,240 --> 00:10:46,550
where there's different
competing language standards,

262
00:10:46,550 --> 00:10:49,820
or there's very high
variance in different regions

263
00:10:49,820 --> 00:10:51,290
on how languages are spoken.

264
00:10:51,290 --> 00:10:54,080
And so this step is a
pretty critical one.

265
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:56,180
So then what we do is
we take the document.

266
00:10:56,180 --> 00:10:58,340
We send it to one
group of translators,

267
00:10:58,340 --> 00:11:00,740
and they do the first
translation step.

268
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:03,110
Then we do some
automatic checking.

269
00:11:03,110 --> 00:11:05,030
If the input
sentence was 10 words

270
00:11:05,030 --> 00:11:07,010
and the output
sentence is 300 words,

271
00:11:07,010 --> 00:11:09,860
it's like, most likely
something went wrong.

272
00:11:09,860 --> 00:11:11,420
And so we send that back.

273
00:11:11,420 --> 00:11:15,290
Otherwise, we'll send it onwards
to a separate, completely

274
00:11:15,290 --> 00:11:19,070
independent set of
translators that do review.

275
00:11:19,070 --> 00:11:21,290
And so they try to rate
the quality of this.

276
00:11:21,290 --> 00:11:23,660
And if the quality doesn't
pass a sufficient bar,

277
00:11:23,660 --> 00:11:26,330
it gets sent back to the
original set of translators

278
00:11:26,330 --> 00:11:27,500
to edit.

279
00:11:27,500 --> 00:11:30,650
And they go through and
address all of the feedback.

280
00:11:30,650 --> 00:11:34,430
And then if it's good enough,
then it enters our data set.

281
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:36,410
And so there's many
challenges here.

282
00:11:36,410 --> 00:11:37,880
The first one, of
course, is just

283
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:41,360
finding translators, and also
finding more translators.

284
00:11:41,360 --> 00:11:44,870
There was a certain issue
that we ran into, for example,

285
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:47,930
that in a certain country, the
internet was not available.

286
00:11:47,930 --> 00:11:51,290
And so it's a lot
of recruitment.

287
00:11:51,290 --> 00:11:54,200
The other one, of course,
is language standardization.

288
00:11:54,200 --> 00:11:56,090
I think I briefly
mentioned this before,

289
00:11:56,090 --> 00:12:00,260
but there's a lot of different
challenges in just understanding

290
00:12:00,260 --> 00:12:02,480
like, what is a
high-quality translation?

291
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:04,970
For example, the
low-resource language Breton.

292
00:12:04,970 --> 00:12:08,360
There's two competing groups
on how do you write Breton.

293
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,090
So it's very difficult to
resolve some of those things.

294
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:12,920
And the final thing
is that there's

295
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:17,480
actually a lot of variation,
even in languages like Arabic.

296
00:12:17,480 --> 00:12:21,860
Moroccan Arabic is very
different from Jordanian Arabic

297
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:22,550
and so on.

298
00:12:22,550 --> 00:12:24,230
And there are also
certain regions

299
00:12:24,230 --> 00:12:25,880
that they speak
the same language.

300
00:12:25,880 --> 00:12:29,150
But due to historical reasons,
they write in different scripts.

301
00:12:29,150 --> 00:12:30,890
And so one of the
things we actually did

302
00:12:30,890 --> 00:12:33,590
was like, if there are languages
written in multiple scripts,

303
00:12:33,590 --> 00:12:36,740
we actually supported the
collection of a multiple script

304
00:12:36,740 --> 00:12:37,610
evaluation.

305
00:12:37,610 --> 00:12:40,340
And I think this is really
important because if you're

306
00:12:40,340 --> 00:12:41,930
building an
underlying technology

307
00:12:41,930 --> 00:12:44,450
and you only choose
one, then I think

308
00:12:44,450 --> 00:12:48,080
you risk just naturally
supporting one over the other,

309
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:51,680
when we really should be a more
neutral technology provider.

310
00:12:51,680 --> 00:12:54,030
And so this is something
that we export a lot

311
00:12:54,030 --> 00:12:57,390
as well as exploring
different variants of Arabic.

312
00:12:57,390 --> 00:12:58,440
This is also open-source.

313
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:00,148
If you just go to this
link, you can just

314
00:13:00,148 --> 00:13:03,540
download all of the
text files for this.

315
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:05,970
With evaluation done, I
want to talk a little bit

316
00:13:05,970 --> 00:13:08,880
about how we collected some
of these training data sets.

317
00:13:08,880 --> 00:13:10,560
The first thing I
want to talk about

318
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:13,650
is this data set we
created called NLLB-Seed,

319
00:13:13,650 --> 00:13:16,590
and the idea of this is,
it's a really seed data

320
00:13:16,590 --> 00:13:19,590
set of high-quality
translations and languages

321
00:13:19,590 --> 00:13:20,970
that really don't have anything.

322
00:13:20,970 --> 00:13:21,540
Why?

323
00:13:21,540 --> 00:13:23,880
Because, well, you can't
start from nothing.

324
00:13:23,880 --> 00:13:26,070
You got to bootstrap
from somewhere.

325
00:13:26,070 --> 00:13:30,450
A lot of people have been using
the Bible as a way to bootstrap,

326
00:13:30,450 --> 00:13:34,140
but it's very limited domain,
obviously, very religious text.

327
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:37,620
And so we created this
data set, NLLB-Seed,

328
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:40,470
for languages that really
don't have anything

329
00:13:40,470 --> 00:13:41,370
to get started from.

330
00:13:41,370 --> 00:13:44,100
It's only about 5,000 sentences,
so it's nothing crazy.

331
00:13:44,100 --> 00:13:46,500
But it supports a lot
of different use cases,

332
00:13:46,500 --> 00:13:48,780
like training language
identification models

333
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:51,760
or sentence encoders,
n-gram language models,

334
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:53,760
all of these things that
I'm about to talk about

335
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,010
in our data set pipeline.

336
00:13:56,010 --> 00:13:59,740
So it covers 43 languages,
about 6,000 sentences.

337
00:13:59,740 --> 00:14:02,410
And the way we decide
to sample it is focused

338
00:14:02,410 --> 00:14:03,730
on really general content.

339
00:14:03,730 --> 00:14:06,310
So Wikipedia has this
article of like, hey,

340
00:14:06,310 --> 00:14:08,320
if you're going to start
like a new Wikipedia

341
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:09,400
in your new language--

342
00:14:09,400 --> 00:14:13,750
I think Wikipedia has 309-ish,
Wikipedia's last I checked.

343
00:14:13,750 --> 00:14:16,370
Here's a list of articles
that every Wikipedia

344
00:14:16,370 --> 00:14:17,620
in a new language should have.

345
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:20,530
And so that's where we sampled
this original content from.

346
00:14:20,530 --> 00:14:22,570
And, of course, it's
also open-source

347
00:14:22,570 --> 00:14:24,670
if you want to download it.

348
00:14:24,670 --> 00:14:28,660
So what we ended up doing to
get large-scale training data

349
00:14:28,660 --> 00:14:30,430
is using mining.

350
00:14:30,430 --> 00:14:33,250
So this is not something we
pioneered in this project.

351
00:14:33,250 --> 00:14:35,560
We have a bunch of
different previous work.

352
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:37,850
So we started from WikiMatrix.

353
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:40,630
We were like, hey, there's
a lot of different sentences

354
00:14:40,630 --> 00:14:42,700
in Wikipedia on
different languages

355
00:14:42,700 --> 00:14:44,170
that we should be
able to match up.

356
00:14:44,170 --> 00:14:46,240
And so we try to do
that with Wikipedia

357
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:49,360
to get machine
translation training data.

358
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:52,360
We extended that to the web
in the CCMatrix project.

359
00:14:52,360 --> 00:14:55,300
And then we extended it to
very, very large scale mining

360
00:14:55,300 --> 00:14:57,430
on all cross pairs
in this project

361
00:14:57,430 --> 00:14:59,920
on beyond English-centric
multilingual machine

362
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:00,660
translation.

363
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:04,120
We really tried to ditch English
as a central pivot language.

364
00:15:04,120 --> 00:15:06,340
And so the way this
whole data mining

365
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:09,490
thing works is that it
focuses on sentence alignment.

366
00:15:09,490 --> 00:15:11,590
So everyone is probably
super familiar with this

367
00:15:11,590 --> 00:15:13,673
because this is how language
models are built now.

368
00:15:13,673 --> 00:15:17,020
It's like, you take common crawl
or any other open-source dump

369
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:17,560
of the web--

370
00:15:17,560 --> 00:15:19,720
I don't know-- like
RedPajama, or whatever

371
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:22,150
you want, CCNet, whatever
you want to use these days.

372
00:15:22,150 --> 00:15:24,370
And you take all of the data.

373
00:15:24,370 --> 00:15:25,960
You extract all of the text.

374
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:28,630
A lot of HTML parsing,
and so on, goes into it.

375
00:15:28,630 --> 00:15:31,840
And the idea is that we want to
try to find matching text that

376
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:33,010
could be a translation.

377
00:15:33,010 --> 00:15:35,170
So we shatter it
all into sentences.

378
00:15:35,170 --> 00:15:38,320
We embed them with different
sentence encoder models.

379
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:40,600
And then we do a match
to try to understand

380
00:15:40,600 --> 00:15:44,500
in a multilingual space
if the sentences match.

381
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:47,050
And so one of the biggest
challenges to this

382
00:15:47,050 --> 00:15:49,570
is that the quality of
the sentence encoding

383
00:15:49,570 --> 00:15:50,530
is very important.

384
00:15:50,530 --> 00:15:52,720
So if your sentence encoding
is not very accurate,

385
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:55,720
then it's impossible to match
in this dimensional space

386
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:58,430
the idea of the
meaning being the same.

387
00:15:58,430 --> 00:16:01,540
And so one of the big things we
try to do here in this project

388
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:05,260
was try to improve the quality
of the sentence encoders.

389
00:16:05,260 --> 00:16:07,390
And so one of the big
things that we did

390
00:16:07,390 --> 00:16:10,270
was train sentence encoders
with masked language modeling.

391
00:16:10,270 --> 00:16:11,410
You see that on the left.

392
00:16:11,410 --> 00:16:14,890
But we also use
multilingual distillation,

393
00:16:14,890 --> 00:16:16,330
which you see on the right.

394
00:16:16,330 --> 00:16:19,900
And so previous approaches
to sentence encoders

395
00:16:19,900 --> 00:16:22,150
and the trend in the research
community for a while

396
00:16:22,150 --> 00:16:26,230
was to really try to embed all
languages in the same sentence

397
00:16:26,230 --> 00:16:27,070
encoder model.

398
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:30,940
So projects like XLM-R, for
example, are in that direction,

399
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:33,040
I think, is pretty widely used.

400
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:34,960
The challenge with
this when you're

401
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:37,090
training a low-resource
model is that a lot

402
00:16:37,090 --> 00:16:40,090
of your high-resource
data just overwhelms

403
00:16:40,090 --> 00:16:41,860
your low-resource data.

404
00:16:41,860 --> 00:16:44,620
And so you don't end up with
a very high-quality sentence

405
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:46,180
encoder for those languages.

406
00:16:46,180 --> 00:16:48,910
So what we ended up doing is
we had a multilingual teacher

407
00:16:48,910 --> 00:16:52,390
model, and we distilled
a bunch of student models

408
00:16:52,390 --> 00:16:56,650
that are specialized to
different language families that

409
00:16:56,650 --> 00:16:57,520
are low resource.

410
00:16:57,520 --> 00:17:00,250
And so this enables the
quality to be pretty high.

411
00:17:00,250 --> 00:17:01,840
So the way that
distillation works

412
00:17:01,840 --> 00:17:04,510
is that the teacher and
the student model both

413
00:17:04,510 --> 00:17:05,349
see the same data.

414
00:17:05,349 --> 00:17:09,130
And then we try to minimize the
cosine loss between the sentence

415
00:17:09,130 --> 00:17:12,285
embeddings that they produce.

416
00:17:12,285 --> 00:17:14,410
I think an important question
that you can ask here

417
00:17:14,410 --> 00:17:17,680
is like, why do you need to
do multilingual distillation?

418
00:17:17,680 --> 00:17:21,280
Why can't you just train a bunch
of different student models,

419
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:22,960
like one per language family?

420
00:17:22,960 --> 00:17:24,849
Why even care
about distillation?

421
00:17:24,849 --> 00:17:26,920
And the reason is
because if you're

422
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:29,740
going to use a bunch of
sentence encoders for mining,

423
00:17:29,740 --> 00:17:32,890
the important thing is that they
all exist in the same embedding

424
00:17:32,890 --> 00:17:34,272
space.

425
00:17:34,272 --> 00:17:36,730
If you train one separate model
and another separate model,

426
00:17:36,730 --> 00:17:38,950
there's nothing
constraining them

427
00:17:38,950 --> 00:17:41,830
so that you can mine all of
the data against each other.

428
00:17:41,830 --> 00:17:43,780
And so one of the
things we found

429
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:46,540
is that by starting everything
from the same teacher model

430
00:17:46,540 --> 00:17:48,940
and trying to use this
cosine loss to minimize

431
00:17:48,940 --> 00:17:50,740
the distance between
embeddings, you

432
00:17:50,740 --> 00:17:53,410
are able to have this
constrained space where

433
00:17:53,410 --> 00:17:56,020
you can mine every language
against every other,

434
00:17:56,020 --> 00:17:58,590
even if you have
different student models.

435
00:17:58,590 --> 00:18:01,860
And so this graph
on the y-axis, it

436
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:04,830
shows the error rate of mining.

437
00:18:04,830 --> 00:18:06,360
And so lower is better.

438
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:08,460
And on the x-axis,
it shows a bunch

439
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:09,960
of different
low-resource languages.

440
00:18:09,960 --> 00:18:11,700
So for example, the
first one is Urdu.

441
00:18:11,700 --> 00:18:14,040
The second one is Telugu.

442
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:15,780
Third one is Tagalog, and so on.

443
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:19,560
And so the gray bar here is
the original laser paper.

444
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:22,410
So this is a paper we put
out maybe in 2018-ish,

445
00:18:22,410 --> 00:18:24,180
and we had all of
these languages.

446
00:18:24,180 --> 00:18:25,500
We counted them as included.

447
00:18:25,500 --> 00:18:27,030
But as you can
see, the error rate

448
00:18:27,030 --> 00:18:29,970
is extremely, extremely
high for these languages.

449
00:18:29,970 --> 00:18:32,010
So even though
they were included,

450
00:18:32,010 --> 00:18:34,200
couldn't really be
used for high quality.

451
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:36,810
And the blue bar
is the laser model

452
00:18:36,810 --> 00:18:39,480
that we trained based on the
technique I just described

453
00:18:39,480 --> 00:18:40,530
in the previous slide.

454
00:18:40,530 --> 00:18:41,250
And you can see that--

455
00:18:41,250 --> 00:18:42,930
I think the most important
point is that you can barely

456
00:18:42,930 --> 00:18:43,860
see the blue bars.

457
00:18:43,860 --> 00:18:47,250
So it was very effective, even
for these previous languages

458
00:18:47,250 --> 00:18:51,360
that people had thought we
had previously embedded.

459
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:53,460
And then so now, how
does this kind of thing

460
00:18:53,460 --> 00:18:56,310
fit into a whole data
pipeline around this approach?

461
00:18:56,310 --> 00:18:58,720
So one of the most
important things

462
00:18:58,720 --> 00:19:01,300
is when you download
the data from the web,

463
00:19:01,300 --> 00:19:04,120
you don't really know
what language it's in.

464
00:19:04,120 --> 00:19:06,700
And so this is part of all
of the large-scale data

465
00:19:06,700 --> 00:19:10,300
cleaning that goes into training
large language models today.

466
00:19:10,300 --> 00:19:13,630
And so the way we identify
different languages is,

467
00:19:13,630 --> 00:19:15,580
is through simple
classification models

468
00:19:15,580 --> 00:19:18,160
called Language
Identification Models.

469
00:19:18,160 --> 00:19:20,710
And I think it's a
classification model.

470
00:19:20,710 --> 00:19:24,400
And so people think it's
easier than it actually is.

471
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:26,410
But I think some of
the major challenges

472
00:19:26,410 --> 00:19:28,990
are that there are so
many different languages.

473
00:19:28,990 --> 00:19:30,730
They're written in
many different ways,

474
00:19:30,730 --> 00:19:33,760
and web text is very casual.

475
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:36,190
And so it can be very
difficult to actually train

476
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:38,800
a good classification model
that can generalize to that.

477
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:43,420
And so what we did is we
had our LID training data.

478
00:19:43,420 --> 00:19:47,200
And we produced a Language
Identification Model, LID.

479
00:19:47,200 --> 00:19:51,070
And then we actually did human
evaluation to label errors

480
00:19:51,070 --> 00:19:53,890
coming from the LID system
to iteratively improve

481
00:19:53,890 --> 00:19:56,830
this on web text itself,
to improve the quality

482
00:19:56,830 --> 00:19:58,480
of the specific model.

483
00:19:58,480 --> 00:20:00,580
Then after we produce
this LID model,

484
00:20:00,580 --> 00:20:02,290
then we insert all
of our Common Crawl

485
00:20:02,290 --> 00:20:04,270
where the web
arrow is coming in.

486
00:20:04,270 --> 00:20:06,520
And we do a ton of
filtering and cleaning.

487
00:20:06,520 --> 00:20:10,420
And this produces a huge corpus
of different monolingual data

488
00:20:10,420 --> 00:20:13,780
that you can then use
for training anything.

489
00:20:13,780 --> 00:20:16,457
Afterwards, we
train our encoder,

490
00:20:16,457 --> 00:20:18,040
what I described on
the previous text.

491
00:20:18,040 --> 00:20:20,290
And then we convert
this monolingual data

492
00:20:20,290 --> 00:20:22,240
into what we call Mined Bitexts.

493
00:20:22,240 --> 00:20:25,120
So these are a huge data
set of things that we think

494
00:20:25,120 --> 00:20:27,580
are translations of each other.

495
00:20:27,580 --> 00:20:29,500
And then, finally, what
we do is we actually

496
00:20:29,500 --> 00:20:33,640
try to validate that these are
real Mined Bitexts by training

497
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:39,550
very small bilingual translation
models in order to see

498
00:20:39,550 --> 00:20:40,780
what the quality is like.

499
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:44,140
And I think this is important
because the data development

500
00:20:44,140 --> 00:20:48,190
cycle and the end task that
it's being used for, you don't

501
00:20:48,190 --> 00:20:51,010
want to completely separate it.

502
00:20:51,010 --> 00:20:53,050
An analogy to large
language model training

503
00:20:53,050 --> 00:20:56,080
today is that when you're
doing your pre-training,

504
00:20:56,080 --> 00:20:58,930
you don't want someone to
just deliver you a data.

505
00:20:58,930 --> 00:21:01,660
The data mix of your different
data sets is very important.

506
00:21:01,660 --> 00:21:04,250
And it's pretty similar here.

507
00:21:04,250 --> 00:21:06,020
And I think one
of the highlights

508
00:21:06,020 --> 00:21:08,420
that we did here
is really focused

509
00:21:08,420 --> 00:21:12,050
on the human evaluation of the
language identification model

510
00:21:12,050 --> 00:21:14,270
because that actually
improves the quality of all

511
00:21:14,270 --> 00:21:17,180
of the underlying data if
you just more accurately know

512
00:21:17,180 --> 00:21:19,320
what language it's in.

513
00:21:19,320 --> 00:21:21,570
And this entire data
pipeline is actually

514
00:21:21,570 --> 00:21:22,920
open-source in this library.

515
00:21:22,920 --> 00:21:25,178
And we had an NLP
paper describing it.

516
00:21:25,178 --> 00:21:26,970
The reason why I thought
this was important

517
00:21:26,970 --> 00:21:29,520
is that because I think
data cleaning is actually

518
00:21:29,520 --> 00:21:31,590
such a fundamental
underlying thing that

519
00:21:31,590 --> 00:21:34,440
drives model quality and
people's data pipelines.

520
00:21:34,440 --> 00:21:36,540
It's like I have this
script and this other thing,

521
00:21:36,540 --> 00:21:37,422
and this other thing.

522
00:21:37,422 --> 00:21:38,880
And so it's actually,
I think, very

523
00:21:38,880 --> 00:21:42,090
important to be able to
recreate it and rerun it

524
00:21:42,090 --> 00:21:45,390
as part of almost
your research that you

525
00:21:45,390 --> 00:21:47,560
would do as follow-up work.

526
00:21:47,560 --> 00:21:50,460
And so this is why
we open-sourced it.

527
00:21:50,460 --> 00:21:53,440
A few reflection things.

528
00:21:53,440 --> 00:21:55,480
For low-resource
languages, even though we

529
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:58,390
did a large-scale mining,
I think monolingual data

530
00:21:58,390 --> 00:21:59,560
is the limiting factor.

531
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:01,420
There are many
languages that do not

532
00:22:01,420 --> 00:22:04,510
have a huge amount of
text written online.

533
00:22:04,510 --> 00:22:06,820
And so it can be very
challenging to get

534
00:22:06,820 --> 00:22:07,900
a large amount.

535
00:22:07,900 --> 00:22:10,510
Further, I think languages
and unique scripts

536
00:22:10,510 --> 00:22:14,440
can be extremely hard to get
good representations of if you

537
00:22:14,440 --> 00:22:16,240
don't have very much data.

538
00:22:16,240 --> 00:22:18,220
There are certain languages
as well where they

539
00:22:18,220 --> 00:22:20,230
were historically
written in a new script,

540
00:22:20,230 --> 00:22:21,910
but now the
government would like

541
00:22:21,910 --> 00:22:25,690
to write it in a totally new
one, like the Ol Chiki script,

542
00:22:25,690 --> 00:22:26,500
for example.

543
00:22:26,500 --> 00:22:28,870
And so there's not
a lot of content

544
00:22:28,870 --> 00:22:30,520
to represent these
scripts, so it's

545
00:22:30,520 --> 00:22:32,200
hard to learn representations.

546
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:34,450
And then further, a
lot of the content

547
00:22:34,450 --> 00:22:38,650
we create, even after mining,
it's a fairly limited domain,

548
00:22:38,650 --> 00:22:41,770
often religious content.

549
00:22:41,770 --> 00:22:45,760
So with data discussed, I
want to segue a little bit

550
00:22:45,760 --> 00:22:49,930
into some of the modeling
work, just to start

551
00:22:49,930 --> 00:22:51,700
with a high-level picture.

552
00:22:51,700 --> 00:22:54,170
I think there's three
major challenges when

553
00:22:54,170 --> 00:22:56,840
you talk about large-scale
multilingual modeling,

554
00:22:56,840 --> 00:23:01,620
and these pretty much apply
to language models as well.

555
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:04,080
The first one is effective
data augmentation

556
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:05,580
for low-resource languages.

557
00:23:05,580 --> 00:23:09,060
How can you prevent the
low-resource language data

558
00:23:09,060 --> 00:23:10,980
from just being
completely drowned out

559
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:14,700
by the time you've seen all of
your words of German or Russian?

560
00:23:14,700 --> 00:23:18,120
I think there's also a question
of scalability of the model.

561
00:23:18,120 --> 00:23:20,970
So even if you train
very large-scale models,

562
00:23:20,970 --> 00:23:22,620
how do you prevent
the representations

563
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:25,470
of different languages from
interfering with each other?

564
00:23:25,470 --> 00:23:27,870
And that leads to the last
point as well of like,

565
00:23:27,870 --> 00:23:30,570
if you give the model
very limited capacity,

566
00:23:30,570 --> 00:23:32,460
then, of course, it may
not have the capacity

567
00:23:32,460 --> 00:23:34,800
to model all of these
different languages.

568
00:23:34,800 --> 00:23:36,900
And so you also
need to accelerate

569
00:23:36,900 --> 00:23:38,940
the scale of the model.

570
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:44,975
And so preliminary for those who
may not have seen a translation

571
00:23:44,975 --> 00:23:45,600
system before--

572
00:23:45,600 --> 00:23:47,790
I don't know how many of
you that practically is--

573
00:23:47,790 --> 00:23:50,490
so we use standard
sequence-to-sequence models.

574
00:23:50,490 --> 00:23:52,590
So the input text,
the choral thing,

575
00:23:52,590 --> 00:23:55,530
is what you want to translate,
enters a transformer decoder

576
00:23:55,530 --> 00:23:56,520
model.

577
00:23:56,520 --> 00:23:58,470
That, then, with the
attention mechanism,

578
00:23:58,470 --> 00:24:00,360
goes to a transformer
decoder model.

579
00:24:00,360 --> 00:24:02,910
And then it decodes
autoregressively

580
00:24:02,910 --> 00:24:06,780
the actual translation, which
you can see here in yellow.

581
00:24:06,780 --> 00:24:10,890
And so I want to talk a little
bit about how the data looks

582
00:24:10,890 --> 00:24:12,313
as we feed it into the models.

583
00:24:12,313 --> 00:24:13,980
So there's a few
different ways that you

584
00:24:13,980 --> 00:24:15,272
might want to think about data.

585
00:24:15,272 --> 00:24:18,360
So you want to be like,
OK, did a human look at it

586
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:21,030
and decide that these two
sentences are translations?

587
00:24:21,030 --> 00:24:22,380
Or are they noisy?

588
00:24:22,380 --> 00:24:24,965
Also, is it limited in size?

589
00:24:24,965 --> 00:24:26,340
Another thing you
can think about

590
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:29,340
is, is the data quality
dependent on some other factor?

591
00:24:29,340 --> 00:24:31,200
And so that's the
model-dependent thing.

592
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:32,670
In which case, the
data quality may

593
00:24:32,670 --> 00:24:36,600
be capped by the quality
of that dependency.

594
00:24:36,600 --> 00:24:40,050
And so I think you can think a
little bit the ideal data set.

595
00:24:40,050 --> 00:24:43,440
It would be like, humans have
reviewed every bit of it.

596
00:24:43,440 --> 00:24:44,730
It's not noisy at all.

597
00:24:44,730 --> 00:24:47,190
We have an infinite
amount, and it

598
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:49,410
doesn't have any dependencies
on any other models.

599
00:24:49,410 --> 00:24:50,940
It's just pure quality.

600
00:24:50,940 --> 00:24:54,300
But in reality, closer to
what we have are these.

601
00:24:54,300 --> 00:24:56,340
So we have a bunch of
different data sources.

602
00:24:56,340 --> 00:24:59,970
We have the seed data that I
discussed way back in the talk,

603
00:24:59,970 --> 00:25:03,060
where it's a small amount
of really high-quality,

604
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:05,400
human-aligned data,
but the only problem

605
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:06,910
is that it's limited in size.

606
00:25:06,910 --> 00:25:09,490
It's 6,000 sentences
per language.

607
00:25:09,490 --> 00:25:11,380
We have the PublicBitext.

608
00:25:11,380 --> 00:25:14,410
So this is data that people
have created over many years

609
00:25:14,410 --> 00:25:15,580
of working in translation.

610
00:25:15,580 --> 00:25:19,810
You can download it from the
opus corpus, for example,

611
00:25:19,810 --> 00:25:22,390
mostly has not been
reviewed by humans,

612
00:25:22,390 --> 00:25:24,190
so pretty extremely noisy.

613
00:25:24,190 --> 00:25:26,860
And many languages, it's
just coming from the Bible,

614
00:25:26,860 --> 00:25:28,540
so the size is quite limited.

615
00:25:28,540 --> 00:25:31,450
You have our Mined data.

616
00:25:31,450 --> 00:25:35,020
So this is not
human-aligned either,

617
00:25:35,020 --> 00:25:36,700
but it does have a
model dependency.

618
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:39,490
It's dependent on the quality
of the sentence encoders.

619
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:43,600
And we have two other sources
of data from back translation.

620
00:25:43,600 --> 00:25:45,700
So the idea of back
translation, it's

621
00:25:45,700 --> 00:25:47,830
a model augmentation
technique heavily used

622
00:25:47,830 --> 00:25:49,510
in machine
translation, where you

623
00:25:49,510 --> 00:25:54,190
use a model to produce pseudo
translations like silver data.

624
00:25:54,190 --> 00:25:55,990
And we use two
different techniques

625
00:25:55,990 --> 00:25:57,970
to produce these back
translations that

626
00:25:57,970 --> 00:26:00,700
also are dependent on
the underlying model used

627
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:02,350
to make the translations.

628
00:26:02,350 --> 00:26:04,720
So this is a picture of a
high-level different data

629
00:26:04,720 --> 00:26:06,370
sources and how
you want to think

630
00:26:06,370 --> 00:26:08,805
about the quality and
the different axes.

631
00:26:08,805 --> 00:26:10,930
And so if we put them all
together, what do we get?

632
00:26:10,930 --> 00:26:14,590
So the y-axis here is the
number of training pairs,

633
00:26:14,590 --> 00:26:18,160
and the x-axis here is the
languages sorted by resource.

634
00:26:18,160 --> 00:26:20,560
So you can see on
the left-hand side,

635
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:22,720
you have your low-resource
languages like Wolof.

636
00:26:22,720 --> 00:26:24,850
And on your right-hand
side, you've

637
00:26:24,850 --> 00:26:27,220
got your high-resource
languages like French.

638
00:26:27,220 --> 00:26:29,410
The peak is English, of course.

639
00:26:29,410 --> 00:26:32,140
And so if you just look at
what's available publicly,

640
00:26:32,140 --> 00:26:34,000
this is a distribution you get.

641
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:37,870
And you'll see a huge, huge
fall-off pretty quickly.

642
00:26:37,870 --> 00:26:42,010
And then if you add in the data
that we have created from mining

643
00:26:42,010 --> 00:26:44,020
and back translation,
our goal is basically

644
00:26:44,020 --> 00:26:47,440
to make the distribution
a little bit more uniform.

645
00:26:47,440 --> 00:26:49,840
It's very hard on the
extremely low resource

646
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:53,500
side, of course, but to make
it a little bit more uniform so

647
00:26:53,500 --> 00:26:56,830
that you don't just immediately
overfit on your low resource

648
00:26:56,830 --> 00:26:59,260
languages before
you've even seen three

649
00:26:59,260 --> 00:27:02,020
shards of your German data.

650
00:27:02,020 --> 00:27:04,840
With that kind of
data strategy in mind,

651
00:27:04,840 --> 00:27:08,140
I want to talk a little bit
about mixture of experts.

652
00:27:08,140 --> 00:27:11,080
So this is something that we
explored quite aggressively

653
00:27:11,080 --> 00:27:14,110
in the translation space
for a number of years.

654
00:27:14,110 --> 00:27:16,223
We could have this
equal conversation

655
00:27:16,223 --> 00:27:17,890
about some of the
debates going on like,

656
00:27:17,890 --> 00:27:20,230
do you want sparse or
dense, architectures

657
00:27:20,230 --> 00:27:21,910
for large language models?

658
00:27:21,910 --> 00:27:24,100
But essentially,
mixture of experts

659
00:27:24,100 --> 00:27:26,770
enables massive
scale because you

660
00:27:26,770 --> 00:27:30,280
don't have to just scale
your dense trunk model.

661
00:27:30,280 --> 00:27:32,770
But you can have a bunch of
different separate experts

662
00:27:32,770 --> 00:27:35,680
that you activate per token.

663
00:27:35,680 --> 00:27:38,680
It also allows you to
avoid language interference

664
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:40,840
because the idea is that
the different experts,

665
00:27:40,840 --> 00:27:43,990
they could specialize
to specific languages.

666
00:27:43,990 --> 00:27:46,120
Unfortunately, it adds
a ton of capacity,

667
00:27:46,120 --> 00:27:48,760
so it becomes pretty
easy to overfit.

668
00:27:48,760 --> 00:27:52,060
So I'm going to talk a little
bit about this overfitting

669
00:27:52,060 --> 00:27:52,720
phenomenon.

670
00:27:52,720 --> 00:27:56,140
So the top set of graphs that
we're going to talk about

671
00:27:56,140 --> 00:27:58,600
is for the language Congo.

672
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:01,820
And then the bottom set
of languages is French.

673
00:28:01,820 --> 00:28:04,820
So you really want to compare
a low-resource language on top

674
00:28:04,820 --> 00:28:06,890
with a high-resource
language on bottom.

675
00:28:06,890 --> 00:28:10,153
So if you just take your dense
model, traditional transformer

676
00:28:10,153 --> 00:28:11,570
sequence-to-sequence
architecture,

677
00:28:11,570 --> 00:28:13,700
that's this graph
that you're showing.

678
00:28:13,700 --> 00:28:15,590
So there's a little
bit of overfitting

679
00:28:15,590 --> 00:28:17,990
on the low-resource language,
but you can pretty much

680
00:28:17,990 --> 00:28:20,630
regularize this with
standard dropout techniques.

681
00:28:20,630 --> 00:28:23,020
So there's not a big
problem and on French.

682
00:28:23,020 --> 00:28:26,010
You basically have
no real problem.

683
00:28:26,010 --> 00:28:28,830
However, the minute you switch
from like a dense architecture

684
00:28:28,830 --> 00:28:31,350
to a token-level
MoE architecture,

685
00:28:31,350 --> 00:28:33,960
you just experience
a massive overfitting

686
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:35,250
on the low-resource language.

687
00:28:35,250 --> 00:28:39,150
So the green line here is just
demonstrating without dropout

688
00:28:39,150 --> 00:28:39,900
the overfitting.

689
00:28:39,900 --> 00:28:42,900
And then if you add dropout,
you get a little bit better

690
00:28:42,900 --> 00:28:46,200
performance, but it's still
overfitting quite a bit,

691
00:28:46,200 --> 00:28:49,510
essentially by 12k updates.

692
00:28:49,510 --> 00:28:51,630
There's no real point
in continuing training.

693
00:28:51,630 --> 00:28:54,180
You're burning GPU, basically.

694
00:28:54,180 --> 00:28:56,640
And so one of the things we
actually worked on quite a bit

695
00:28:56,640 --> 00:29:00,900
was trying to figure out how to
properly regularize these MoE

696
00:29:00,900 --> 00:29:04,080
architectures with this
specific masking technique

697
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:08,460
on the gating function that
decides which expert to route

698
00:29:08,460 --> 00:29:11,880
to in your MoE architecture,
to just try to pull back some

699
00:29:11,880 --> 00:29:13,170
of this overfitting effect.

700
00:29:13,170 --> 00:29:17,010
So if you look in the top-right
graph, the purple line,

701
00:29:17,010 --> 00:29:22,860
you still see some
successful regularization.

702
00:29:22,860 --> 00:29:26,370
Another thing that we did
to control the overfitting

703
00:29:26,370 --> 00:29:29,130
effect-- it's actually quite
being used in language models

704
00:29:29,130 --> 00:29:31,530
today as well-- is
curriculum learning.

705
00:29:31,530 --> 00:29:34,080
And the idea of
this is, how are we

706
00:29:34,080 --> 00:29:37,080
going to stage when
languages are introduced?

707
00:29:37,080 --> 00:29:40,440
And so what we did was we
tried to train a vanilla model.

708
00:29:40,440 --> 00:29:42,210
And then we started
to measure when

709
00:29:42,210 --> 00:29:43,830
the languages begin to overfit.

710
00:29:43,830 --> 00:29:47,430
And then we basically bucket
them into different sections.

711
00:29:47,430 --> 00:29:50,100
And so for high-resource
languages like French,

712
00:29:50,100 --> 00:29:51,240
you want to start it early.

713
00:29:51,240 --> 00:29:53,220
And it needs to be
trained the entire way.

714
00:29:53,220 --> 00:29:56,310
But for a lower resource
language like Wolof,

715
00:29:56,310 --> 00:29:59,100
after maybe like 100k
updates, it's done.

716
00:29:59,100 --> 00:30:01,433
So the rest of the time,
it's just overfitting.

717
00:30:01,433 --> 00:30:03,600
And so it actually gets
worse the more you train it.

718
00:30:03,600 --> 00:30:05,892
So what we did is we moved
some of those lower resource

719
00:30:05,892 --> 00:30:08,400
languages, and we
inserted them much later

720
00:30:08,400 --> 00:30:09,630
into the training schedule.

721
00:30:09,630 --> 00:30:11,430
So you start training
your high resource,

722
00:30:11,430 --> 00:30:13,775
then you start training
your mid-resource, and then

723
00:30:13,775 --> 00:30:15,900
your low resource, and then
your very low resource.

724
00:30:15,900 --> 00:30:18,570
And so by the end,
everything in theory

725
00:30:18,570 --> 00:30:21,540
has trained and is not
as overfit as it would be

726
00:30:21,540 --> 00:30:23,810
without this kind of technique.

727
00:30:23,810 --> 00:30:25,550
So I want to show some results.

728
00:30:25,550 --> 00:30:28,670
So first, I want to show
results on existing data sets.

729
00:30:28,670 --> 00:30:30,890
So before we get
to 200 languages,

730
00:30:30,890 --> 00:30:32,840
let's just talk
about 100 languages.

731
00:30:32,840 --> 00:30:35,600
And so this is the
FLORES-101 devtest

732
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:37,880
it's important to compare
to this because this

733
00:30:37,880 --> 00:30:41,180
is where existing benchmarks
in the community lie.

734
00:30:41,180 --> 00:30:44,210
Whereas on 200, of course,
we can put up anything

735
00:30:44,210 --> 00:30:46,050
because it's the
first work on that.

736
00:30:46,050 --> 00:30:51,050
So the first column is
translating out of English.

737
00:30:51,050 --> 00:30:53,660
So English to Chinese,
English to Icelandic,

738
00:30:53,660 --> 00:30:54,530
anything like that.

739
00:30:54,530 --> 00:30:57,140
The second column is
translating into English.

740
00:30:57,140 --> 00:30:58,610
So Chinese to English.

741
00:30:58,610 --> 00:31:03,200
The third column, xx-yy, is
translating any cross pair

742
00:31:03,200 --> 00:31:04,430
not involving English.

743
00:31:04,430 --> 00:31:06,410
And the last column
is the average.

744
00:31:06,410 --> 00:31:08,480
So if you look at the
first set of rows,

745
00:31:08,480 --> 00:31:10,190
this is a comparison
on models that

746
00:31:10,190 --> 00:31:12,200
cover 87 different languages.

747
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:14,510
So there was this
paper, M2M-100.

748
00:31:14,510 --> 00:31:16,400
There was also
this Deepnet paper.

749
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:18,680
So you can see the
average blue score.

750
00:31:18,680 --> 00:31:20,800
Blue is a standard
translation metric,

751
00:31:20,800 --> 00:31:23,330
essentially a metric
of word overlap.

752
00:31:23,330 --> 00:31:25,800
So we're looking
at blue score here.

753
00:31:25,800 --> 00:31:29,270
And so you can see the last
row, and it will be 200.

754
00:31:29,270 --> 00:31:31,460
Even though we
cover 200 languages,

755
00:31:31,460 --> 00:31:35,030
the blue score is substantially
above some of the existing work.

756
00:31:35,030 --> 00:31:37,370
Now, if we look
at 101 languages,

757
00:31:37,370 --> 00:31:40,130
only the DeltaLM paper
from Microsoft at the time

758
00:31:40,130 --> 00:31:42,050
covered that number
of languages.

759
00:31:42,050 --> 00:31:45,620
And so if you compare on all
of the different cross sets,

760
00:31:45,620 --> 00:31:48,560
similarly, you see that this
no language left behind.

761
00:31:48,560 --> 00:31:51,800
Model is much stronger
in terms of blue.

762
00:31:51,800 --> 00:31:54,530
One thing, really quick, on the
variance of these blue numbers,

763
00:31:54,530 --> 00:31:56,360
I think it's important
to understand,

764
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,280
is something statistically
significant or not?

765
00:31:58,280 --> 00:32:03,650
I think about 0.5 blue
is the general plus-minus

766
00:32:03,650 --> 00:32:04,370
that you'll see.

767
00:32:04,370 --> 00:32:07,850
And so if it's above
that, it's usually

768
00:32:07,850 --> 00:32:11,630
a statistically
significant improvement.

769
00:32:11,630 --> 00:32:14,660
So now, I want to talk a little
bit about FLORES-200 results.

770
00:32:14,660 --> 00:32:17,990
So here are similar, the first
chunk of columns translating out

771
00:32:17,990 --> 00:32:18,710
of English.

772
00:32:18,710 --> 00:32:21,260
Then next chunk is
translating into English.

773
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:22,970
Then you have your cross pairs.

774
00:32:22,970 --> 00:32:25,410
And then you have your average.

775
00:32:25,410 --> 00:32:27,840
So we have this
blue metric as well.

776
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:30,690
We also have a
character-level metric,

777
00:32:30,690 --> 00:32:34,830
based on chrF plus plus that's
commonly used in the translation

778
00:32:34,830 --> 00:32:35,470
community.

779
00:32:35,470 --> 00:32:37,470
So I think, looking at
these numbers, of course,

780
00:32:37,470 --> 00:32:39,660
there's no baseline work
to compare to unlike

781
00:32:39,660 --> 00:32:40,770
on the previous slide.

782
00:32:40,770 --> 00:32:43,980
And so when we get to human
evaluation and a little bit,

783
00:32:43,980 --> 00:32:45,480
it'll be more concrete.

784
00:32:45,480 --> 00:32:49,050
But I think, generally,
one of the rules of thumb

785
00:32:49,050 --> 00:32:51,810
I have for these types
of numbers is around 30

786
00:32:51,810 --> 00:32:56,790
is pretty reasonably
becomes usable.

787
00:32:56,790 --> 00:32:58,710
And I think another
thing, like if you compare

788
00:32:58,710 --> 00:33:02,460
these supervised pairs
to zero-shot pairs,

789
00:33:02,460 --> 00:33:04,680
I think we don't
see a huge drop off

790
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:06,720
on zero-shot, which
indicates the model has

791
00:33:06,720 --> 00:33:08,670
some sort of
generalization, even

792
00:33:08,670 --> 00:33:11,310
if it didn't see that
translation pair directly

793
00:33:11,310 --> 00:33:12,780
during training.

794
00:33:12,780 --> 00:33:14,820
Another way to
calibrate some of this

795
00:33:14,820 --> 00:33:16,830
is to compare to
Google Translate.

796
00:33:16,830 --> 00:33:19,350
And so if you compare
to Google Translate,

797
00:33:19,350 --> 00:33:21,420
No Language Left Behind
is quite a bit better

798
00:33:21,420 --> 00:33:25,290
at translating into English, and
not as good as translating out

799
00:33:25,290 --> 00:33:25,920
of English.

800
00:33:25,920 --> 00:33:28,230
Although if you like
average across everything,

801
00:33:28,230 --> 00:33:31,480
it's a little bit better.

802
00:33:31,480 --> 00:33:33,610
I want to talk a little
bit about human evaluation

803
00:33:33,610 --> 00:33:36,700
as well to complement
some of our discussion

804
00:33:36,700 --> 00:33:38,590
on automatic evaluation.

805
00:33:38,590 --> 00:33:40,870
And so I think
automatic metrics--

806
00:33:40,870 --> 00:33:43,660
fast, really good for
research iteration,

807
00:33:43,660 --> 00:33:45,190
impossible to move
forward without.

808
00:33:45,190 --> 00:33:49,310
But human evaluation is
really the real deal here.

809
00:33:49,310 --> 00:33:52,330
And so we had this
paper at AMTA on how

810
00:33:52,330 --> 00:33:55,810
to make this human evaluation
very consistent and scalable

811
00:33:55,810 --> 00:33:57,400
across different language pairs.

812
00:33:57,400 --> 00:34:00,850
I think this goes back to
the evaluation data set point

813
00:34:00,850 --> 00:34:03,190
that I was making at the
beginning of the talk, where

814
00:34:03,190 --> 00:34:05,917
if you're a professional
German translator,

815
00:34:05,917 --> 00:34:07,750
you're really good at
evaluating the quality

816
00:34:07,750 --> 00:34:09,760
of your German translation.

817
00:34:09,760 --> 00:34:13,750
But beyond that, there's
not a lot of consistency.

818
00:34:13,750 --> 00:34:18,190
And if you evaluate translation
on a five-point scale,

819
00:34:18,190 --> 00:34:20,596
like a five translating
between two languages

820
00:34:20,596 --> 00:34:22,929
and like a three translating
between other two languages

821
00:34:22,929 --> 00:34:24,790
like, are those
really comparable?

822
00:34:24,790 --> 00:34:27,610
And so we had this entire
experiment methodology

823
00:34:27,610 --> 00:34:30,620
on how we might want to
make this a little bit more

824
00:34:30,620 --> 00:34:32,179
comparable.

825
00:34:32,179 --> 00:34:35,340
So I want to show some
results now on this.

826
00:34:35,340 --> 00:34:37,230
So the y-axis here--

827
00:34:37,230 --> 00:34:40,489
so the metric is called XSTS,
some metric for how we're

828
00:34:40,489 --> 00:34:42,050
doing this human evaluation.

829
00:34:42,050 --> 00:34:44,254
The y-axis here is
actually the delta.

830
00:34:44,254 --> 00:34:46,940

831
00:34:46,940 --> 00:34:51,980
It's a five-point scale, so
it's a delta, not the raw score.

832
00:34:51,980 --> 00:34:55,250
The x-axis here is a bunch of
different translation directions

833
00:34:55,250 --> 00:34:56,420
that we evaluated.

834
00:34:56,420 --> 00:34:59,600
So the gray set is
translating into English.

835
00:34:59,600 --> 00:35:03,230
The green set is translating
non-English directions,

836
00:35:03,230 --> 00:35:06,860
so like French to Wolof.

837
00:35:06,860 --> 00:35:09,860
And then the blue set is
translating out of English.

838
00:35:09,860 --> 00:35:13,250
And so what you're looking
for is a positive delta,

839
00:35:13,250 --> 00:35:16,970
indicates that our modeling
architecture is much better.

840
00:35:16,970 --> 00:35:22,190
So what the delta is between is
a baseline transformer model,

841
00:35:22,190 --> 00:35:25,700
just trained on all of our data,
versus the final No Language

842
00:35:25,700 --> 00:35:27,350
Left Behind model
that we created.

843
00:35:27,350 --> 00:35:29,660
So the data is actually
the same for both of them.

844
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:32,090
That's how we get
all 200 languages.

845
00:35:32,090 --> 00:35:34,310
So we're just measuring
here the human eval

846
00:35:34,310 --> 00:35:36,170
of the modeling improvements.

847
00:35:36,170 --> 00:35:41,330
And so you can see most of the
delta is pretty noticeable.

848
00:35:41,330 --> 00:35:45,030
Some of them, not so much.

849
00:35:45,030 --> 00:35:45,910
I don't know.

850
00:35:45,910 --> 00:35:48,400
Zulu to English, we didn't
seem to improve very much.

851
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:50,470
But in general,
it's an improvement

852
00:35:50,470 --> 00:35:52,240
detectable by human evaluation.

853
00:35:52,240 --> 00:35:55,210
You might also ask, OK, what is
the statistically significant

854
00:35:55,210 --> 00:35:59,140
difference here between
about 0.2 to 0.3

855
00:35:59,140 --> 00:36:02,530
plus or minus is something
that's pretty noticeable.

856
00:36:02,530 --> 00:36:06,790
And above 0.5, it's
very noticeable.

857
00:36:06,790 --> 00:36:10,450
One of the things that I also
want to get at in evaluation

858
00:36:10,450 --> 00:36:15,550
is that there's many different
facets of model evaluation.

859
00:36:15,550 --> 00:36:17,980
And I think if you look at
all of the different LLM

860
00:36:17,980 --> 00:36:20,170
leaderboards or the
transparency reports

861
00:36:20,170 --> 00:36:23,320
or whatever, you begin to
internalize this pretty quickly.

862
00:36:23,320 --> 00:36:26,560
But what we just looked at are
just very high-level summary

863
00:36:26,560 --> 00:36:28,480
numbers, and they
don't really tell you

864
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:30,220
what exactly are the errors.

865
00:36:30,220 --> 00:36:32,440
And is it ultimately
usable by people?

866
00:36:32,440 --> 00:36:35,170
Is it a safe thing that
people can rely on?

867
00:36:35,170 --> 00:36:37,510
And so one of the things
we really focused on

868
00:36:37,510 --> 00:36:39,280
is user safety.

869
00:36:39,280 --> 00:36:42,760
And some of that manifests
in some of the toxicity

870
00:36:42,760 --> 00:36:43,820
work that we did.

871
00:36:43,820 --> 00:36:46,720
And the driving thing here
is that not all errors

872
00:36:46,720 --> 00:36:48,310
in translation are made equal.

873
00:36:48,310 --> 00:36:49,930
So during COVID,
there was this one

874
00:36:49,930 --> 00:36:52,690
that really went viral
circulating around,

875
00:36:52,690 --> 00:36:55,320
but the message during COVID
is you got to wash your hands.

876
00:36:55,320 --> 00:36:57,070
But the translation
producer was like, you

877
00:36:57,070 --> 00:37:00,400
got to hold hands, which I think
is like exactly the opposite

878
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:02,050
of what you want to do.

879
00:37:02,050 --> 00:37:03,730
And other types of
measurement errors

880
00:37:03,730 --> 00:37:05,480
are really important as well.

881
00:37:05,480 --> 00:37:08,150
So if you're telling someone
how far they want to go

882
00:37:08,150 --> 00:37:10,640
and you're like, hey, you
want to travel 5 kilometers,

883
00:37:10,640 --> 00:37:13,820
and then your translation
is travel 500 kilometers,

884
00:37:13,820 --> 00:37:16,550
it's a completely
different type of issue.

885
00:37:16,550 --> 00:37:19,010
And so what we did
for toxicity, which

886
00:37:19,010 --> 00:37:21,320
is a big focus for
this work, is that we

887
00:37:21,320 --> 00:37:25,490
collected different toxicity
lists for all 200 languages.

888
00:37:25,490 --> 00:37:27,800
And so why do I care
so much about toxicity?

889
00:37:27,800 --> 00:37:29,750
I think it's a
user safety thing.

890
00:37:29,750 --> 00:37:32,450
So if you input some
perfectly benign text

891
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:34,400
and then the output
is profanity,

892
00:37:34,400 --> 00:37:36,560
I think it's just like
really unexpected,

893
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:38,990
and it breaks a lot of
trust in the system.

894
00:37:38,990 --> 00:37:41,810
And it's an extremely poor
experience for people.

895
00:37:41,810 --> 00:37:45,080
That, being said, it's also a
very, very challenging thing

896
00:37:45,080 --> 00:37:47,660
because it's extremely
culturally specific.

897
00:37:47,660 --> 00:37:52,220
So things that are slurs or
insults in certain languages,

898
00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:55,670
they don't really
generalize across cultures,

899
00:37:55,670 --> 00:37:57,680
which means that
things like this

900
00:37:57,680 --> 00:38:00,240
are very challenging to create.

901
00:38:00,240 --> 00:38:02,240
And I also was very
interested in this direction

902
00:38:02,240 --> 00:38:04,880
because I think it's
broadly useful for all sorts

903
00:38:04,880 --> 00:38:08,120
of different type of detection,
things that you need to do,

904
00:38:08,120 --> 00:38:09,410
and also mitigation.

905
00:38:09,410 --> 00:38:10,970
And so even though
we develop this

906
00:38:10,970 --> 00:38:12,800
in the context of
translation, it

907
00:38:12,800 --> 00:38:17,780
can be used very broadly in
other types of NLP applications.

908
00:38:17,780 --> 00:38:19,070
This is also open-source.

909
00:38:19,070 --> 00:38:19,940
You can download it.

910
00:38:19,940 --> 00:38:22,190
You have to type in a
little password that's

911
00:38:22,190 --> 00:38:24,980
in the github repo just so
that you don't accidentally

912
00:38:24,980 --> 00:38:27,590
download and realize you
have files of curse words

913
00:38:27,590 --> 00:38:30,570
all over your computer.

914
00:38:30,570 --> 00:38:32,700
So I want to end a little
bit with some thoughts

915
00:38:32,700 --> 00:38:34,500
about future directions.

916
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:40,110
And before I get there, there's
a 190-page paper that writes up

917
00:38:40,110 --> 00:38:44,930
all of this in far greater
detail in case you're curious.

918
00:38:44,930 --> 00:38:47,300
So a few future
directions that I

919
00:38:47,300 --> 00:38:50,420
think I'm really interested
in, and some of these

920
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:52,820
are also very applicable
to things like speech,

921
00:38:52,820 --> 00:38:57,350
is that I think one of them
is more explicit multilingual.

922
00:38:57,350 --> 00:39:00,290
So I think a lot of
approaches to multilingual

923
00:39:00,290 --> 00:39:02,930
have been like, hey, we
have this thing that's

924
00:39:02,930 --> 00:39:04,370
working well for one language.

925
00:39:04,370 --> 00:39:07,275
Let's try to scale it to a
bunch of different languages.

926
00:39:07,275 --> 00:39:09,650
And then we're going to put
them all in the same modeling

927
00:39:09,650 --> 00:39:12,650
bucket and just hope
that the model learns

928
00:39:12,650 --> 00:39:14,450
all of these different
representations.

929
00:39:14,450 --> 00:39:18,920
But I think there's a lot of
potential room for explicitly

930
00:39:18,920 --> 00:39:22,100
bringing in, like the fact that
you know it's multilingual,

931
00:39:22,100 --> 00:39:25,100
and so the architecture more.

932
00:39:25,100 --> 00:39:32,000
And so it's possible to capture
more nuances between languages

933
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:35,450
or different relationships
between languages.

934
00:39:35,450 --> 00:39:37,910
And the other one is continued
support for everyone.

935
00:39:37,910 --> 00:39:40,760
I think it's like something
reflecting on this project,

936
00:39:40,760 --> 00:39:45,240
is that going from 100 to 200
was already pretty challenging,

937
00:39:45,240 --> 00:39:47,310
but going beyond a
lot of the techniques

938
00:39:47,310 --> 00:39:51,150
that we developed here are
not necessarily that scalable.

939
00:39:51,150 --> 00:39:53,370
This is actually what
inspired some of our work

940
00:39:53,370 --> 00:39:55,270
on speech translation as well.

941
00:39:55,270 --> 00:39:58,260
So if you recently saw
the seamless M42 release

942
00:39:58,260 --> 00:40:00,300
or the unwritten
languages-- we did

943
00:40:00,300 --> 00:40:01,680
a lot of modeling of Hoquiam.

944
00:40:01,680 --> 00:40:03,870
And I think that goes
into this direction

945
00:40:03,870 --> 00:40:07,230
really well because many of
the languages that people want

946
00:40:07,230 --> 00:40:10,140
to use are spoken first
languages, and not

947
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:12,810
necessarily primarily written.

948
00:40:12,810 --> 00:40:14,970
And then I think the
last thing that I'm still

949
00:40:14,970 --> 00:40:18,660
really passionate about is
continued increase ease of use

950
00:40:18,660 --> 00:40:21,600
and training of these
models, and democratization

951
00:40:21,600 --> 00:40:22,770
for the community.

952
00:40:22,770 --> 00:40:26,010
So one of the things that we
tried to do in this work is just

953
00:40:26,010 --> 00:40:29,320
really, really clearly write
down everything that we did

954
00:40:29,320 --> 00:40:32,667
and open-source, even the data
pipeline and things like that.

955
00:40:32,667 --> 00:40:34,500
And so that's where you
get all of the repos

956
00:40:34,500 --> 00:40:37,950
that I linked in,
like a huge write-up.

957
00:40:37,950 --> 00:40:39,390
But I think if
someone were to try

958
00:40:39,390 --> 00:40:42,090
to reproduce this for their own
language-- and many people have.

959
00:40:42,090 --> 00:40:44,070
I'm not saying that hasn't been.

960
00:40:44,070 --> 00:40:46,440
But it's like, if you
wanted to do this,

961
00:40:46,440 --> 00:40:49,320
it would be extremely,
extremely hard

962
00:40:49,320 --> 00:40:52,290
because there's just like so
much different things going on.

963
00:40:52,290 --> 00:40:54,210
So I think most
of what we've seen

964
00:40:54,210 --> 00:40:55,980
is people have
downloaded the base model

965
00:40:55,980 --> 00:40:57,870
and fine-tuned it for
their own language.

966
00:40:57,870 --> 00:40:59,610
But it's pretty
hard to just like

967
00:40:59,610 --> 00:41:03,090
add on many, many more
languages to the system

968
00:41:03,090 --> 00:41:05,880
because of how complicated
all of the moving parts are.

969
00:41:05,880 --> 00:41:08,910
And so I feel like something
for the translation community

970
00:41:08,910 --> 00:41:13,290
overall is, how do we simplify
a lot of these things?

971
00:41:13,290 --> 00:41:15,960
And I think that's where a
lot of fundamental modeling

972
00:41:15,960 --> 00:41:18,810
innovation could help us get to.

973
00:41:18,810 --> 00:41:21,390
And so, yeah, I got a
chance to give this talk,

974
00:41:21,390 --> 00:41:23,280
but, of course, the
work is like being

975
00:41:23,280 --> 00:41:27,120
done by a huge team of
people that I've cited here.

976
00:41:27,120 --> 00:41:31,440
And yeah, if you want to use any
of this or read more about it,

977
00:41:31,440 --> 00:41:33,990
everything is linked from
this main github repo

978
00:41:33,990 --> 00:41:35,760
here in fairseq.

979
00:41:35,760 --> 00:41:39,420
And you can click on
everything else afterwards.

980
00:41:39,420 --> 00:41:42,630
But yeah, maybe I'll
go back to Steven

981
00:41:42,630 --> 00:41:45,130
if we have any questions
or anything else like that.

982
00:41:45,130 --> 00:41:45,630
All right.

983
00:41:45,630 --> 00:41:46,940
No, thanks for the great talk.

984
00:41:46,940 --> 00:41:48,570
Yeah, if anybody
has any questions,

985
00:41:48,570 --> 00:41:50,310
feel free to unmute and ask.

986
00:41:50,310 --> 00:41:57,220

987
00:41:57,220 --> 00:42:00,520
Did you consult with a
lot of native speakers

988
00:42:00,520 --> 00:42:04,300
for profanities in
this type of stuff?

989
00:42:04,300 --> 00:42:09,310
How were you able to get access
