1095
00:46:28,740 --> 00:46:32,520
So things like French or
German will work reasonably.

1096
00:46:32,520 --> 00:46:36,250

1097
00:46:36,250 --> 00:46:37,300
So just to clarify.

1098
00:46:37,300 --> 00:46:42,320
You said language models trained
with a translation objective

1099
00:46:42,320 --> 00:46:45,912
do better because--

1100
00:46:45,912 --> 00:46:46,870
They tend to do better.

1101
00:46:46,870 --> 00:46:49,150
If you fine-tune for
the translation task,

1102
00:46:49,150 --> 00:46:50,695
it will tend to do better.

1103
00:46:50,695 --> 00:46:52,750
Well, that makes sense
compared to, for example,

1104
00:46:52,750 --> 00:46:57,290
some few-shot
in-context examples.

1105
00:46:57,290 --> 00:46:57,790
Exactly.

1106
00:46:57,790 --> 00:46:58,990
Exactly.

1107
00:46:58,990 --> 00:47:03,910
Now, one other
question is, do you

1108
00:47:03,910 --> 00:47:05,590
see this being similar
to, for example,

1109
00:47:05,590 --> 00:47:08,830
fine-tuning on particular
expert domains, which might also

1110
00:47:08,830 --> 00:47:14,440
have less data and
low resource, and as

1111
00:47:14,440 --> 00:47:18,490
well as domain-specific
jargon and so forth?

1112
00:47:18,490 --> 00:47:18,990
Yeah.

1113
00:47:18,990 --> 00:47:21,780
I mean, I think if we were
